 ## 4. Strategic responses and innovation
 
-- **Launch prices.** Shanmugam et al. 2026, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2026.17742](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2026.17742)) (press): no measurable change in the launch-price trend after the IRA.
-- **Trials and the "pill penalty".** Industry-associated studies report large drops in small-molecule investment or trials (the National Pharmaceutical Council cites a 68% fall in early-stage venture funding; individual studies and their funding are listed in [academic.md](ira_notes/academic.md), not re-verified here); Kang et al. 2026, Health Affairs ([10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00720](https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00720)) find overall trial activity stable, with fewer trials only at manufacturers with negotiated drugs. Wouters et al. 2024, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2024.16911](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2024.16911)) find little evidence that biologics need the longer protection. Funding sources matter here and are recorded in [academic.md](ira_notes/academic.md).
+- **Launch prices.** Shanmugam et al. 2026, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2026.17742](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2026.17742)) (full text): among 643 new drugs approved 2008–2025, launch prices rose about 7.8% a year before the IRA, with no significant change in level or trend after September 2022 (trend change −3.7% a year, 95% CI −23.6 to 21.4).
+- **Trials and the "pill penalty".** Industry-funded studies report large drops: Schulthess et al. 2025 (Vital Transformation, Ther Innov Regul Sci, [10.1007/s43441-025-00773-3](https://doi.org/10.1007/s43441-025-00773-3), full text) find aggregate early-stage venture investment in small molecules fell 68% from its pre-IRA peak ($2 billion to $640 million by June 2024) across 161 lead assets; other industry-associated studies are listed in [academic.md](ira_notes/academic.md); Kang et al. 2026, Health Affairs ([10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00720](https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00720)) find overall trial activity stable, with fewer trials only at manufacturers with negotiated drugs. Wouters et al. 2024, JAMA ([10.1001/jama.2024.16911](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2024.16911)) find little evidence that biologics need the longer protection. Funding sources matter here and are recorded in [academic.md](ira_notes/academic.md).
 - **Deselection.** Beinfeld et al. 2025 model the overlap of Stelara's MFP and biosimilars; Castle et al. 2026 (JAMA Network Open) describe 2026 prices and coverage during that overlap. Neither separates the effect of negotiation from that of biosimilar competition.
 
