-**2. On the day the MFPs began, four of the ten negotiated drugs took large list-price cuts, and so did some related non-selected brands.** NADAC records WAC-based cuts effective 2026-01-01 of 43% for Eliquis, 44% for Jardiance, 37% for Farxiga and 75% for Fiasp (the CMS NovoLog entry covers NovoLog and Fiasp; NovoLog itself had been cut 75% on 2024-01-01). Januvia, and Janumet in the 2027 group, had been cut 42% on 2025-01-01. In January 2026, 30% of the brand NDCs of the 2026 drugs had a cut of 20% or more, against 1.5% of other brand NDCs. Most of those other-brand cuts are not independent: 30 of the 33 are Boehringer Ingelheim's empagliflozin combinations, cut by the same 44.4% on the same day as Jardiance, and three are Tresiba; through February, Lilly's 70% cuts on Lyumjev and Humalog U-200 (2026-02-01) join them. List-price increases were as common for the 2026 drugs as for other brands once February is included (46% of their NDCs, from Xarelto on 2026-02-03 and Enbrel, Entresto and Stelara in January, against 39%). Imbruvica, which has no NADAC, also cut its list price on 2025-12-22 according to NCPA's notice of WAC decreases, so six of the ten have had list-price cuts since 2024. These are NADAC changes that CMS attributes to WAC changes, not a separate WAC series; 46brooklyn's independent WAC data show the same Eliquis (−43.0%) and Jardiance (−44.4%) cuts. Insulin list-price cuts also cluster around the removal of the Medicaid rebate cap on 2024-01-01, so the MFP is one of several candidate explanations; these data cannot separate them.
+**2. On the day the MFPs began, four of the ten negotiated drugs took large list-price cuts, and so did some related non-selected brands.** NADAC records WAC-based cuts effective 2026-01-01 of 43% for Eliquis, 44% for Jardiance, 37% for Farxiga and 75% for Fiasp (the CMS NovoLog entry covers NovoLog and Fiasp; NovoLog itself had been cut 75% on 2024-01-01). Januvia, and Janumet in the 2027 group, had been cut 42% on 2025-01-01. In January 2026, 30% of the brand NDCs of the 2026 drugs had a cut of 20% or more, against 1.5% of other brand NDCs. Most of those other-brand cuts are not independent: 30 of the 33 are Boehringer Ingelheim's empagliflozin combinations, cut by the same 44.4% on the same day as Jardiance, and three are Tresiba; through February, Lilly's 70% cuts on Lyumjev and Humalog U-200 (2026-02-01) join them. List-price increases were as common for the 2026 drugs as for other brands once February is included (46% of their NDCs, from Xarelto on 2026-02-03 and Enbrel, Entresto and Stelara in January, against 39%). Imbruvica, which has no NADAC, also cut its list price on 2025-12-22 according to NCPA's notice of WAC decreases, so six of the ten have had list-price cuts since 2024. These are NADAC changes that CMS attributes to WAC changes, not a separate WAC series; 46brooklyn's independent WAC data show the same Eliquis (−43.0%) and Jardiance (−44.4%) cuts. Two documented mechanisms make such cuts cheap for manufacturers: Medicaid and 340B inflation-based rebates (Bristol Myers Squibb told Reuters the Eliquis cut avoids inflation penalties; the Pennsylvania Office of Rural Health reports that the cuts ended penny pricing in 340B from 2026-07-01, e.g. Eliquis $245 a package), and, for insulin, the removal of the Medicaid rebate cap on 2024-01-01. The MFP is one of several candidate explanations; these data cannot separate them.
 
 **3. NADAC remains well above the MFP for most of these drugs.** For brand-classified NDCs, NADAC, the average invoice cost of retail community pharmacies, was 2.2 to 4.0 times the 2026 MFP per unit at the end of December 2025, except NovoLog, already at 1.0 after its 2024 cut. By October 2026, after the January list-price cuts, it was 0.99 times for Fiasp, 1.33 for Eliquis, 1.65 for Jardiance and 2.00 for Farxiga. Januvia stayed at 2.70, and Xarelto (2.84), Entresto (2.22), Stelara (3.32) and Enbrel (3.57) rose slightly, by 2% to 5%, with their 2026 list-price increases. Ratios use the MFP in effect on each snapshot date (CMS corrected the Entresto Sprinkle MFP from 2026-06-01) and agree at the displayed precision on NDCs present in both snapshots. The CMS list also covers unbranded and authorized-generic NDCs (Novo Nordisk's insulin aspart, dapagliflozin) that carry generic NADAC rates and their own MFPs; they are reported separately in the summary tables.
 
